# 6. LOCATION MODEL

Create a reusable location model.

A location may contain:

```text
id
tenant_id
organization_id
branch_id
name
latitude
longitude
radius_meters
accuracy_requirement
status
created_at
updated_at
created_by
updated_by
```

The exact fields should follow existing architecture.

Do not store unnecessary personal location history.

---

# 7. BRANCH LOCATION

Integrate location configuration with Phase 3 branches.

Example:

```text
Branch
  |
  +-- Office Location
  |
  +-- Latitude
  +-- Longitude
  +-- Geofence
```

A branch should optionally have one or more attendance locations.

Support future scenarios such as:

```text
Head Office
Site Office
Warehouse
Factory
Client Location
Remote Work Location
```

---

# 8. GEOFENCE

Implement geofence management.

A basic geofence should support:

```text
center latitude
center longitude
radius
```

Example:

```text
Office:
28.4595
77.0266

Radius:
150 meters
```

Employee is considered inside if the calculated distance is within the configured radius, subject to accuracy/business rules.

---

# 9. GEO-FENCE TYPES

Design an extensible structure supporting:

```text
CIRCULAR
POLYGON
```

Phase 5 may initially implement:

```text
CIRCULAR
```

but the architecture should not make polygon geofencing impossible later.

Future:

```text
Polygon
      ↓
Office boundary
      ↓
Employee must be inside exact boundary
```

---

# 10. GEOFENCE ASSIGNMENT

Allow authorized administrators to assign locations/geofences to:

* Branch
* Department
* Employee
* Attendance policy

The precedence rule must be clearly defined.

Example:

```text
Employee-specific geofence
        ↓
Department geofence
        ↓
Branch geofence
        ↓
Organization default
```

Do not implement multiple conflicting rules without documenting precedence.

---

# 11. LOCATION POLICY

Create a location attendance policy.

Possible configuration:

```text
location_required
geofence_enabled
allowed_radius
minimum_accuracy
location_timeout
location_freshness_seconds
allow_outside_geofence
outside_geofence_action
```

Example:

```text
Location Required: YES
Geofence: YES
Radius: 100 meters
Minimum Accuracy: 50 meters
```

---

# 12. GPS CHECK-IN

Extend Phase 4 check-in.

Example:

```http
POST /api/v1/attendance/check-in
```

Request may contain:

```json
{
  "latitude": 28.4595,
  "longitude": 77.0266,
  "accuracy": 12.5,
  "captured_at": "2026-09-01T09:02:15+05:30"
}
```

Do not blindly trust these values.

The server must validate:

* authenticated employee
* tenant
* timestamp
* coordinate ranges
* accuracy
* location freshness
* geofence
* attendance state
* attendance policy

---

# 13. COORDINATE VALIDATION

Validate:

```text
latitude: -90 to +90
longitude: -180 to +180
accuracy > 0
```

Reject invalid coordinates.

Examples:

```text
latitude = 500        ❌
longitude = -500     ❌
accuracy = -10       ❌
```

---

# 14. LOCATION FRESHNESS

Do not accept an old location indefinitely.

Example:

```text
GPS captured:
09:00

Request:
09:25
```

Depending on policy, the location may be considered stale.

Create configurable:

```text
location_freshness_seconds
```

Example:

```text
60 seconds
120 seconds
300 seconds
```

Do not hard-code one universal value.

---

# 15. GPS ACCURACY

GPS coordinates have an accuracy radius.

Example:

```text
Latitude
Longitude
Accuracy = 8 meters
```

Do not treat GPS coordinates as infinitely precise.

Implement:

```text
minimum acceptable accuracy
```

Example:

```text
Accuracy <= 50m → accepted
Accuracy > 50m → rejected/pending according to policy
```

The exact rule must be configurable.

---

# 16. DISTANCE CALCULATION

Implement server-side distance calculation.

For circular geofences, use a proper geographic distance calculation such as the Haversine formula or an appropriate geospatial library.

Do NOT calculate distance only in JavaScript/frontend.

The backend must be authoritative.

Example:

```text
Employee GPS
     |
     ↓
Distance Calculator
     |
     ↓
Office Location
     |
     ↓
Distance = 43 meters
     |
     ↓
Allowed Radius = 100 meters
     |
     ↓
INSIDE GEOFENCE
```

---

# 17. GEOFENCE DECISION

Possible results:

```text
INSIDE
OUTSIDE
UNKNOWN
INVALID_LOCATION
LOW_ACCURACY
STALE_LOCATION
```

Do not reduce all failures to a generic error.

This information will be important for audit and troubleshooting.

---

# 18. OUTSIDE GEOFENCE POLICY

Organizations may want different behavior.

Support configurable behavior such as:

```text
REJECT
ALLOW_WITH_WARNING
REQUIRE_APPROVAL
RECORD_EXCEPTION
```

Example:

```text
Employee outside office
       ↓
Policy = REJECT
       ↓
Attendance rejected
```

Another tenant:

```text
Employee outside office
       ↓
Policy = ALLOW_WITH_WARNING
       ↓
Attendance accepted
       ↓
Audit exception recorded
```

---

# 19. LOCATION ATTENDANCE EVENT

Extend the attendance event model or create a related location record.

Store appropriate metadata such as:

```text
latitude
longitude
accuracy
captured_at
distance_from_geofence
geofence_id
location_status
location_source
```

Avoid duplicating information unnecessarily.

---

# 20. LOCATION PRIVACY

Location data is sensitive.

Implement strict access control.

Employees should be able to see their own relevant attendance location information.

Administrators/managers should only see location data according to permissions.

Do not expose employee location history to every authenticated user.

---

# 21. LOCATION RETENTION

Design the system so organizations can configure retention policies later.

Do not assume location data must be stored forever.

Separate:

```text
Attendance record
```

from:

```text
Detailed location metadata
```

where practical.

This will make future retention policies easier.

---

# 22. LOCATION AUDIT

Record important location decisions.

Example:

```text
LOCATION_VALIDATED
GEOFENCE_PASSED
GEOFENCE_FAILED
LOW_GPS_ACCURACY
STALE_LOCATION
LOCATION_REQUIRED
LOCATION_REJECTED
```

Audit information should contain enough information for investigation without exposing unnecessary sensitive data.

---

# 23. DEVICE INFORMATION

Prepare device metadata.

Potential fields:

```text
device_id
device_type
platform
platform_version
app_version
device_model
```

Do not collect unnecessary device information.

---

# 24. DEVICE REGISTRATION

Prepare architecture for trusted device management.

Future capability:

```text
Employee
   ↓
Registered Device
   ↓
Attendance
```

Possible fields:

```text
device_id
employee_id
tenant_id
device_type
status
registered_at
last_seen_at
```

Do not make device binding mandatory unless the product requirements explicitly require it.

---

# 25. DEVICE SECURITY

Do not trust a client-provided:

```text
device_id
```

as a strong security credential.

A device identifier is metadata, not authentication.

Use the existing authentication mechanism as the primary identity.

---

# 26. SELFIE VERIFICATION FOUNDATION

Implement the architecture for optional selfie verification.

Do NOT build an insecure custom facial-recognition system.

Create an abstraction:

```text
IdentityVerificationService
```

with future providers such as:

```text
FaceVerificationProvider
ThirdPartyVerificationProvider
InternalVerificationProvider
```

---

# 27. SELFIE ATTENDANCE FLOW

Future-compatible flow:

```text
Employee
   |
   ↓
Check In
   |
   ↓
Capture Selfie
   |
   ↓
Upload Securely
   |
   ↓
Verification Service
   |
   ↓
Verification Result
   |
   ↓
Attendance Engine
```

Phase 5 should establish the interfaces and secure metadata architecture.

---

# 28. SELFIE METADATA

Do not unnecessarily store raw biometric information in PostgreSQL.

Possible metadata:

```text
verification_id
employee_id
attendance_event_id
provider
status
score
reference
verified_at
```

Avoid storing biometric templates unless there is a clear legal, security, and architectural requirement.

---

# 29. VERIFICATION STATUS

Use controlled values:

```text
NOT_REQUIRED
PENDING
VERIFIED
FAILED
REJECTED
EXPIRED
ERROR
```

Attendance behavior must depend on configured policy.

---

# 30. VERIFICATION POLICY

Support:

```text
verification_required
verification_method
minimum_score
failure_action
```

Example:

```text
Selfie Required = YES
Failure Action = REJECT
```

Another policy:

```text
Selfie Required = YES
Failure Action = REQUIRE_REVIEW
```

---

# 31. SELFIE STORAGE

Do NOT store large images directly inside PostgreSQL unless there is a specific reason.

Use object-storage architecture:

```text
PostgreSQL
    |
    └── storage_key/reference

Object Storage
    |
    └── selfie file
```

Future storage:

```text
S3
Azure Blob
Google Cloud Storage
```

For local development, use an appropriate local storage abstraction.

---

# 32. SECURE FILE ACCESS

Selfie/profile images must not be publicly accessible.

Use:

* authenticated access
* signed URLs where appropriate
* authorization checks
* tenant isolation
* expiration
* access logging where necessary

Never expose unrestricted storage URLs for sensitive employee images.

---

# 33. ANTI-SPOOFING FOUNDATION

The system should be designed to reduce obvious spoofing risks.

Consider:

```text
location freshness
GPS accuracy
device metadata
server timestamps
request authentication
duplicate request detection
selfie verification provider
```

Do not claim that GPS alone prevents spoofing.

Do not implement a fake "100% spoof-proof" system.

---

# 34. MOCK VERIFICATION PROVIDER

For development/testing, implement a mock verification provider.

Example:

```text
MockFaceVerificationProvider
```

It should return deterministic test results.

Example:

```text
VERIFIED
FAILED
ERROR
```

This allows automated tests without depending on an external biometric provider.

---

# 35. PROVIDER ABSTRACTION

Use an interface similar to:

```text
VerificationProvider
    |
    +-- MockVerificationProvider
    +-- FutureExternalProvider
```

The attendance engine should not know provider-specific implementation details.

---

# 36. ATTENDANCE VERIFICATION PIPELINE

Design a reusable verification pipeline:

```text
Authentication
      ↓
Tenant Validation
      ↓
Employee Validation
      ↓
Shift Resolution
      ↓
Attendance Policy
      ↓
Location Validation
      ↓
Geofence Validation
      ↓
Identity Verification
      ↓
Attendance State Validation
      ↓
Attendance Event
      ↓
Attendance Summary
      ↓
Audit
```

Not every attendance event must use every verification stage.

The pipeline should be policy-driven.

---

# 37. VERIFICATION DECISION ENGINE

Create a clear decision model.

Example:

```text
Location = INSIDE
GPS Accuracy = GOOD
Selfie = VERIFIED
Shift = VALID
Attendance State = VALID

       ↓

ATTENDANCE ACCEPTED
```

Failure:

```text
Location = OUTSIDE
Policy = REJECT

       ↓

ATTENDANCE REJECTED
```

Another:

```text
Location = OUTSIDE
Policy = ALLOW_WITH_WARNING

       ↓

ATTENDANCE ACCEPTED
+
EXCEPTION RECORDED
```

---

# 38. CHECK-IN API RESPONSE

Return useful information.

Example:

```json
{
  "success": true,
  "data": {
    "attendance_id": "...",
    "event_id": "...",
    "status": "PRESENT",
    "check_in_time": "...",
    "location_status": "INSIDE",
    "verification_status": "VERIFIED"
  }
}
```

Do not expose internal implementation details.

---

# 39. FAILED LOCATION RESPONSE

Example:

```json
{
  "success": false,
  "error": {
    "code": "OUTSIDE_GEOFENCE",
    "message": "You are outside the allowed attendance location."
  }
}
```

Other useful errors:

```text
LOCATION_REQUIRED
INVALID_COORDINATES
LOW_LOCATION_ACCURACY
STALE_LOCATION
GEOFENCE_NOT_CONFIGURED
VERIFICATION_REQUIRED
VERIFICATION_FAILED
```

